WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.100
GABRIELA FERRARA: Welcome.

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.870
In this session, watch as
we deploy an application

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.420
of the utmost importance
and mission critical value,

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.145
a meme generator.

00:00:08.145 --> 00:00:11.869
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:20.400
Memes are serious business, so
we use for our stack Cloud SQL

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.480
and Kubernetes to guarantee the
availability and scalability

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.930
of our application.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.150
Just so you have an
idea, everything Google

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.439
runs within containers.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:36.210
Did you know we deploy around
4 billion containers per week?

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.790
It's impressive number.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:44.010
To achieve such scale, we needed
a reliable tool, Kubernetes.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:47.370
It was developed not to only
solve Google's infrastructure

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.310
challenges, but also to be
accessible to developers.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.730
With its declarative
set of APIs,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:57.390
Kubernetes can run on GKE,
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.300
or even on a multi-cloud
environment outside

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.210
of the Google Cloud Platform--

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:08.970
all of that with auto-repair,
auto-upgrade, and auto-scale.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.610
Cloud SQL is our
persistent database

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:15.490
used to store the generating
memes created by users,

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.820
and it is set up to
have high availability

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.500
across different zones.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.170
But deploying is not the
only objective at hand.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.390
Watch as we delete
nodes and force

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.070
a failover in the database.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:29.070
After all, outages can
happen, and you wouldn't

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.680
want to miss anything, right?

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.900
How about trying by yourself
the deployment and force

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.220
a failover?

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:37.950
You can also take
this opportunity

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.870
to have some fun with memes.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.450
Check out the full talk to
see the deployment steps

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.670
for this application in action.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.510
And don't forget, subscribe
to our channel for more great

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:48.510
Next Rewind content.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:52.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

